@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<http://example.org/sen0414/ch07/diagrams> a owl:Ontology ; rdfs:label "SEN0414 ch07 narrative diagrams - ABox"@en ; owl:versionInfo "1.0.0" ; dcterms:identifier "sen0414_ch07_diagrams_abox_v1_0_0" ;
    rdfs:comment "The chapter's authored diagrams as cme:NarrativeDiagram individuals: generated by sen0414_page_data_v2_6_0.py from sen0414_ch07_diagrams_v1_0_0.py, typed by the CME narrative-to-diagram mapping (a module of cme_standards_adoption_v1_3_0.ttl, CME 0.16.0)."@en ; owl:imports <http://example.org/cme/narrative-diagram> .

chx:Diagram_ReadingAKey a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "Choosing how to read a key from a dictionary"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:Workflow ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:ActivityDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 7, the Reading a pair and Absent keys sections: the in test asks whether a key is present, the get method returns the value or a fallback without changing the dictionary, the setdefault method stores a default only if the key is not there and returns the value now stored, and the subscript raises KeyError when the key is absent" ; cme:elementCount 14 ;
    cme:accompanies "MembershipOnKeys" ; cme:accompanies "GetMethod" ; cme:accompanies "SetdefaultMethod" ; cme:accompanies "KeyErrorOnMissing" ; rdfs:comment "the explanations describe what the programmer decides in order - does the key have to be there, is a fallback enough, must the key exist afterwards - with a different tool on each branch: a workflow with choices on the way"@en .
chx:Diagram_LifeOfAPair a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "The life of a key-value pair in a dictionary"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:LifeCycle ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:StateDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 7, the Adding, replacing and removing section: assignment with square brackets adds a pair when the key is new and replaces the value when the key exists, the del statement removes a pair and raises KeyError when the key is not present, and the pop method removes a pair and returns its value" ; cme:elementCount 10 ;
    cme:accompanies "AddOrReplacePair" ; cme:accompanies "DeletePair" ; cme:accompanies "PopMethod" ; cme:accompanies "KeyValuePair" ; rdfs:comment "the explanations describe the states a pair is in - not in the dictionary, present with a value, present with a replaced value, removed - and the operations that move it from one state to the next: a life cycle"@en .
chx:Diagram_HashedLookup a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "How a dictionary finds the value of a key"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:Interaction ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:SequenceDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 7, the Keys and hashing section: Python turns the key into a number by a rule, uses that number to decide where the pair is kept, compares the key it finds there with the key asked for, and returns the value or raises KeyError; a list cannot be a key because its hash value would change" ; cme:elementCount 13 ;
    cme:accompanies "HashValue" ; cme:accompanies "HashableKeys" ; cme:accompanies "KeyErrorOnMissing" ; cme:accompanies "KeyValuePair" ; rdfs:comment "the passage is an exchange between parties in an order that matters - the program asks, the dictionary computes the hash, the table answers with a stored pair, the keys are compared and the value or the error is returned: an interaction"@en .
chx:Diagram_CountingByKey a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "Counting the items of a sequence with a dictionary"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:Workflow ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:ActivityDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 7, the Setting default values section and the character count program: the loop takes each character, setdefault makes sure the key exists starting at 0, and one is added to the count; the Counter class and the defaultdict class give the same result in less code" ; cme:elementCount 12 ;
    cme:accompanies "CharacterCount" ; cme:accompanies "CounterClass" ; cme:accompanies "DefaultdictCounting" ; rdfs:comment "the explanations describe the steps the program repeats for every item - take the item, make sure its key exists with the count 0, add one - and the choice of tool that shortens them: a workflow with a loop and a decision"@en .
chx:Diagram_ChessboardUses a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "What the chessboard simulator lets its user do"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:SetOfUses ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:UseCaseDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 7, the Interactive Chessboard Simulator project: the user types move, remove, set, reset, clear, fill or quit, the program changes the dictionary that holds the pieces, and after each command the printing function draws the board from the template" ; cme:elementCount 12 ;
    cme:accompanies "ChessboardCommands" ; cme:accompanies "ChessboardModel" ; cme:accompanies "ChessboardPrinter" ; rdfs:comment "the passage lists what the user and the program do with the chessboard dictionary - move a piece, remove it, set a square, reset or clear the board, fill it, see the board drawn - a set of uses by actors, not a sequence"@en .
chx:Diagram_DictionaryAtAGlance a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "A dictionary at a glance: what it is made of and what it can do"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:TopicAndParts ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:MindMap ; cme:readFrom "chapter 7, the opening sections: a dictionary is a mutable collection of key-value pairs written in curly brackets, the keys must be hashable, it differs from a list in having keys and not indexes, the methods keys, values and items return views, and values may themselves be dictionaries" ; cme:elementCount 18 ;
    cme:accompanies "DictionaryType" ; cme:accompanies "KeyValuePair" ; cme:accompanies "HashableKeys" ; cme:accompanies "KeysValuesItems" ; cme:accompanies "NestedDictionaries" ; rdfs:comment "the section classifies one topic, the dictionary, by what it is made of, by the rule for its keys, by how it differs from a list and by the ways of reading it, and lists the choices under each - a topic and its parts, laid out to remember"@en .
chx:Diagram_CopyingChoices a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "Three ways to copy a nested dictionary"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:TopicAndParts ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:MindMap ; cme:readFrom "chapter 7, the Nested Dictionaries and Lists section and the chessboard reset: an assignment makes a second name for the same dictionary, copy.copy makes a new outer dictionary that shares the inner ones, and copy.deepcopy copies every level; the chessboard resets with copy.copy of the starting pieces" ; cme:elementCount 12 ;
    cme:accompanies "CopyingDictionaries" ; cme:accompanies "NestedDictionaries" ; cme:accompanies "ChessboardModel" ; rdfs:comment "the passage lists the choices open to the programmer when a nested dictionary must be copied - an alias, a shallow copy, a deep copy - and what each shares with the original: a topic and its parts, laid out to choose between"@en .
